<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Castilla de Oro</h1><p class="st-meta">1513 CE – 1538 CE (approx.) · capital Panamá</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Spain's first mainland colony in the Americas, on the Isthmus of Panama, from which the Pacific was reached and the conquest of Peru launched.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-nunez-de-balboa">Vasco Núñez de Balboa</a> founded Santa María la <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-antigua">Antigua</a> del Darién in 1510 and crossed the isthmus to sight the Pacific in 1513. That year the Crown named the region Castilla de Oro and appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedrarias-davila">Pedrarias Dávila</a> as governor; he arrived in 1514 with a large expedition.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedrarias-davila">Pedrarias</a> ruled harshly, raiding indigenous communities for gold and slaves. Rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-nunez-de-balboa">Balboa</a> ended with Balboa's execution in 1519. That year Pedrarias founded Panamá on the Pacific coast, which became the capital. Expeditions from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Panama</a> conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> in 1524 and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a>, set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The indigenous population of the isthmus collapsed. In 1538 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/real-audiencia-of-panama">Real Audiencia of Panama</a> was created, replacing the governorship as the main authority on the isthmus.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/castilla-de-oro.jpg" alt="Castilla de Oro" width="320" height="243"><figcaption>Image: Americo Vespuccio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castilla_del_Oro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1513 CE – 1538 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Panamá</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pedrarias Dávila, Vasco Núñez de Balboa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/panama">Panama</a>, <a href="/countries/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>, <a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Castile of Gold, Darién</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1526&amp;focus=castilla-de-oro" aria-label="Castilla de Oro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2460 1852 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Castilla de Oro lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2780 2108h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M2780 2108h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Panama. <a href="/atlas?year=1526&amp;focus=castilla-de-oro">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
